course: dictionary
lesson: 23
title: Absolutely: a word that makes things stronger
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Absolutely» is a small word that makes something stronger. It can mean completely or totally, as in «absolutely right», or it can be a warm, strong «yes» when you agree.
explain: Look at the words around it to find its meaning. In «Absolutely nothing whatever», the Mole means not one thing at all, so absolutely makes nothing even more complete. Leo woke «absolutely certain» a skeleton was under his bed: he was completely sure, not just a little bit sure.
explain: With «not» or «nothing», absolutely makes a strong no, as in «absolutely not». Before a feeling word, like «absolutely starving», it makes the feeling stronger. Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence: does it still make sense?
fact: Absolutely means completely or totally, as in «absolutely right».
fact: «Absolutely!» on its own is a strong way to say yes when you agree.
fact: With «not» or «nothing», absolutely makes a strong no, as in «absolutely not».
fact: In maths, «absolutely certain» means the chance is 1: it is sure to happen.
fact: Absolutely is informal when it is used to stress a feeling, so it fits talk well but may be too casual in a formal letter.
exercise: Leo woke at 3 a.m. «absolutely certain» a skeleton was under his bed. What does absolutely mean here? | Slightly worried | Quietly | Half asleep | *Completely sure
exercise: The Mole said «Absolutely nothing whatever». What does absolutely add to nothing? | Something very large | Something he will tell you later | *Not even one thing, not a single thing | A little bit of something
exercise: Ella asks, «Do you want a biscuit?» Her friend says «Absolutely!» What does she mean? | No, never | I have already eaten one | Maybe, I will think about it | *Yes, I really do want one
test: In «absolutely starving», what does absolutely do to the word starving? | *It makes the feeling much stronger | It turns it into the opposite, so not hungry at all | It makes it sound sleepy | It turns it into a question
test: Mum asks, «Can I stay up until midnight?» You reply «Absolutely not!» What do you mean? | *A strong no | A strong yes | A question about midnight | A maybe, ask me later
exam: In «absolutely right», which meaning fits best? | Slightly, a little | Very quickly | Not at all | *Completely, totally
